### Task 6: Push-subscription opt-in

**Files:**
- Create: `dashboard/public/sw.js`
- Create: `dashboard/src/push/subscribeToPush.ts`
- Create: `dashboard/src/push/PushOptInButton.tsx`
- Modify: `dashboard/src/Layout.tsx` (add the button to the nav bar)

**Interfaces:**
- Produces: `subscribeToPushNotifications(): Promise<void>`, `PushOptInButton`.
- Consumes: `getVapidPublicKey`, `createPushSubscription` (from `api/pushSubscriptions.ts`, already exist).

Per `docs/design.md`'s own words: "browser web push is not self-contained — it routes through Google's FCM, needs HTTPS and a registered service worker, and can't be fully exercised end-to-end without real deployment infrastructure." This task builds the SUBSCRIBE step correctly (service worker registration + `pushManager.subscribe()` + POSTing the subscription to the backend) — that part IS verifiable locally, since browsers exempt `localhost` from the HTTPS requirement for service workers. Actually RECEIVING a push (the backend calling `pywebpush` and it arriving in the browser) is the part that needs real infra and is out of scope for local verification, consistent with every prior phase's treatment of this same caveat.

- [ ] **Step 1: Write the service worker**

```js
// dashboard/public/sw.js
self.addEventListener('push', (event) => {
  const data = event.data ? event.data.json() : {}
  const title = data.type === 'ZoneViolationDetected' ? 'Zone violation detected' : 'Atlas Field Force'
  event.waitUntil(
    self.registration.showNotification(title, {
      body: data.rep_id ? `Rep ${data.rep_id} left their assigned zone` : 'A new alert was received.',
    }),
  )
})
```

This matches the payload shape `notify_managers_of_violation` sends (`backend/app/violations/service.py`) — read that function if you want to confirm the exact keys (`type`, `rep_id`, `zone_id`, `violation_id`, `detected_at`) before writing this.

- [ ] **Step 2: Write `dashboard/src/push/subscribeToPush.ts`**

```ts
// dashboard/src/push/subscribeToPush.ts
import { createPushSubscription, getVapidPublicKey } from '../api/pushSubscriptions'

function urlBase64ToUint8Array(base64String: string): Uint8Array {
  const padding = '='.repeat((4 - (base64String.length % 4)) % 4)
  const base64 = (base64String + padding).replace(/-/g, '+').replace(/_/g, '/')
  const rawData = window.atob(base64)
  return Uint8Array.from([...rawData].map((char) => char.charCodeAt(0)))
}

export async function subscribeToPushNotifications(): Promise<void> {
  if (!('serviceWorker' in navigator) || !('PushManager' in window)) {
    throw new Error('Push notifications are not supported in this browser.')
  }

  const registration = await navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js')
  const { vapid_public_key: vapidPublicKey } = await getVapidPublicKey()
  if (!vapidPublicKey) {
    throw new Error('Push is not configured on the server yet.')
  }

  const subscription = await registration.pushManager.subscribe({
    userVisibleOnly: true,
    applicationServerKey: urlBase64ToUint8Array(vapidPublicKey),
  })

  const raw = subscription.toJSON()
  if (!raw.endpoint || !raw.keys?.p256dh || !raw.keys?.auth) {
    throw new Error('Browser did not return a complete push subscription.')
  }

  await createPushSubscription({
    endpoint: raw.endpoint,
    p256dh: raw.keys.p256dh,
    auth: raw.keys.auth,
  })
}
```

`urlBase64ToUint8Array` exists because VAPID public keys are transmitted as URL-safe base64 strings but `PushManager.subscribe`'s `applicationServerKey` option needs raw bytes (a `Uint8Array`/`BufferSource`) — this is the standard, well-known conversion every Web Push tutorial uses; it is not custom cryptography, just base64 decoding with the URL-safe alphabet normalized back to standard base64 first.

- [ ] **Step 3: Write `dashboard/src/push/PushOptInButton.tsx`**

```tsx
// dashboard/src/push/PushOptInButton.tsx
import { useState } from 'react'
import { subscribeToPushNotifications } from './subscribeToPush'

export function PushOptInButton() {
  const [status, setStatus] = useState<'idle' | 'loading' | 'done' | 'error'>('idle')
  const [errorMessage, setErrorMessage] = useState('')

  async function handleClick() {
    setStatus('loading')
    try {
      await subscribeToPushNotifications()
      setStatus('done')
    } catch (error) {
      setStatus('error')
      setErrorMessage(error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Failed to enable alerts.')
    }
  }

  if (status === 'done') {
    return <span className="text-sm text-green-700">Alerts enabled</span>
  }

  return (
    <div className="flex items-center gap-2">
      <button
        onClick={handleClick}
        disabled={status === 'loading'}
        className="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-gray-900 disabled:opacity-50"
      >
        {status === 'loading' ? 'Enabling…' : 'Enable violation alerts'}
      </button>
      {status === 'error' && <span className="text-xs text-red-600">{errorMessage}</span>}
    </div>
  )
}
```

- [ ] **Step 4: Wire `PushOptInButton` into `dashboard/src/Layout.tsx`**

Read the current file first (from Task 1). Add the import and place `<PushOptInButton />` in the right-hand nav group, alongside the user email and sign-out button:

```tsx
import { PushOptInButton } from './push/PushOptInButton'
```

```tsx
<div className="flex items-center gap-4">
  <PushOptInButton />
  <span className="text-sm text-gray-500">{user?.email}</span>
  <button onClick={logout} className="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-gray-900">
    Sign out
  </button>
</div>
```

- [ ] **Step 5: Verify**

```bash
cd /Users/taha/Documents/atlas-fieldforce/dashboard
npm run typecheck
npm run build
npx vitest run
```

- [ ] **Step 6: Commit**

```bash
cd /Users/taha/Documents/atlas-fieldforce
git add dashboard/
git commit -m "feat(dashboard): push-subscription opt-in (service worker + VAPID subscribe flow)

Refs PLAN 7.4"
```

